course: world
lesson: 163
title: "Because I said so" and other dead-end reasons
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ethan asks why he has to go to bed at eight. Mum: "Because I said so." He asks why again. "Because I'm your mother." That answers "who decides", not "why". It is a stop sign, not a reason.
explain: Other dead ends sound like reasons but go nowhere: "Phones are bad because they're bad" just says the answer twice. "You're not going because I don't like it, and I don't like it because you're not going" is a loop.
explain: Some dead ends lean on age or habit: "I'm the adult, so I'd know", "back in my day we didn't ask". Some jump from one small step to a disaster: blue hair, then tattoos, then the circus.
explain: Parents are allowed to decide. But there usually is a real reason, and saying it helps: children of 9 or 10 need about 9 to 11 hours of sleep to grow and learn, and vegetables give fibre and vitamins.
explain: Kids roll their eyes at the dead end, not at the parent: "Phones are bad because they're bad. Wow. Somebody write that down, it's science." A good adult can laugh and then give the real reason.
fact: "Because I said so" answers who decides, not why; grown-ups call leaning on who you are instead of a reason an appeal to authority.
fact: Saying the answer again in other words as the reason is going in circles; grown-ups call it circular reasoning or begging the question.
fact: Children of 9 or 10 need about 9 to 11 hours of sleep a night to grow and learn.
fact: "That's how we always did it" does not show it is the best way; this trap is called appeal to tradition.
fact: Vegetables give your body fibre and vitamins, which is a real reason to eat peas, today and long ago.
fact: Changing a deal after the other person has kept it is called moving the goalposts.
fact: Treating a small chance as a sure thing, or a small step as the start of a disaster, is called worst-case thinking or the slippery slope.
fact: Adults usually know more, so listening to them is sensible, but a fact is checked by looking, not by asking who is oldest.
fact: How cold it is can be checked with a window, a thermometer or a weather app; one person's feeling is not the measurement.
fact: Hair dye that washes out lasts a few weeks; a parent may still say no, but should give a real reason, such as school rules.
fact: The test of a dishwasher method is whether the dishes come out clean, not whose habit it is; big pans should not block the spray arm.
fact: Words people use: "back in my day" is how older people start a story about the past (Gen X and boomers), "love that for me" is sarcastic for "how useless", "bro got scammed" means he was tricked, "boy" is a Texan grandpa's word for a lad, and "cold-mum" is a family joke word for mums who always feel cold.
exercise: Ethan asks why bedtime is at eight; Mum says, "Because I said so." What does her answer tell him? | Who decides, but not why | Why he needs sleep | That bedtime will change soon | That the rule is only for tonight
exercise: What is a real reason a 9-year-old should go to bed early? | Children that age need about 9 to 11 hours of sleep | Because mothers always know best | Because it gets dark at eight every day | Because adults want to have the TV and the sofa all to themselves
exercise: Dad says phones are bad "because they are". What is the trap? | Saying the answer again as the reason | Thinking phones are good for children | Not knowing what a phone is | Giving too many reasons at once
exercise: Grandpa Joe in Texas says Tyler must eat his peas "because that's how we did it back in my day". What is wrong with that reason? | Old habits can be good or bad, so being old doesn't prove it | Peas were not grown back in his day | It is a good reason because it is old | Grandpas are never allowed to give reasons, so Tyler can ignore him
exercise: What is the real reason to eat peas? | They give fibre and vitamins | Grandpa ate them, so they are right | They make you grow taller overnight | They are the cheapest food there is
exercise: Mum says it's cold "because I'm cold". What is the broken step? | Taking her own feeling as a fact about the weather | Not wearing enough warm jumpers and woolly socks herself | Forgetting that it is July | Thinking coats keep you warm
exercise: Dad said "no dessert till you finish your broccoli", then added carrots after Ollie finished. What is this called? | Moving the goalposts | Going in circles | Appeal to tradition | A fair deal
exercise: Ruby can't play outside "because you'll get hurt". She asks, "Every time?" What trap is Mum in? | Treating a small chance as a sure thing | Thinking outside is always completely safe | Forgetting to buy Ruby a proper bike helmet | Asking Ruby to stay outside much too late at night
exercise: Leo's mum says, "I'm the adult, so I'd know," but she has put salt in the sugar bowl. What proves the fact? | Tasting the tea | Asking who is oldest | Asking Dad to decide | Reading the label on the bowl's lid
exercise: Sienna's dad says blue hair leads to tattoos, dropping out and joining a circus. What is this trap? | The slippery slope: one small step "means" disaster | Going round in circles, saying the very same words again and again | Appeal to tradition | Moving the goalposts after a deal
exercise: Mum: "You're not going because I don't like it." "Why don't you like it?" "Because you're not going." What has Mum made? | A loop, where the reason and the rule point at each other | A fair rule with a clear reason | A deal that Zoe can win | A joke that everyone in the family understands, apart from Zoe
exercise: Dad says plates go at the back "because that's the right way", but Mum's way gets everything just as clean. What decides the right way? | Whether the dishes come out clean | Whose habit is older and has lasted longer | Who is the stronger parent in the house | What the neighbours do with their plates
exercise: What do grown-ups call "I'm in charge, so I'm right"? | Appeal to authority | Appeal to tradition | Moving the goalposts | Going in circles
exercise: What would be a real reason not to give a 9-year-old a phone? | It can take time from sleep and homework, and some apps aren't safe at that age | Phones are bad because they are bad | Because nobody had phones in the old days | Because Dad said so and that's final
exercise: What could Mum say about the party to make it a real reason? | "I'm worried there are no adults and it ends too late." | "Because you're not going, that's why." | "Because I don't like parties full stop." | "Because I'm your mother, end of, and I don't need a reason."
test: Ethan, under his breath: "Love that for me. Very informative." What is he being sarcastic about? | The answer gave him no reason at all | His bedtime being too late | Mum telling him too much | Liking the word "informative"
test: Ava: "Phones are bad because they're bad. Wow. Somebody write that down, it's science." What is she mocking? | The circle of saying the answer as the reason | Her dad for not owning a phone | Science lessons at school | People who write things down instead of remembering them in their heads
test: Tyler: "Back in your day you didn't have the internet, Grandpa." What point is he making? | The old way is not automatically the right way now | The internet is a good reason not to eat your peas | Grandpa is far too old to understand anything at all | Peas were only invented after the internet came along
test: Mia: "Mum, you wear a jumper in July. You're not a thermometer." What should actually decide about the coat? | A check of the real weather, and maybe taking it just in case | Whatever Mum feels at that moment | Whatever Mia feels at that moment | Never wearing coats at all
test: Ollie's sister: "Bro got scammed by his own dad." What does she mean? | Dad changed the deal after Ollie kept his side | Dad sold Ollie's dessert | Ollie tricked Dad out of the carrots | Dad gave Ollie more dessert than he had promised him at first
test: Ruby: "I might get hurt on the stairs too. Should I live in the hallway?" What is the better question for Mum to ask? | What can we do to make playing outside safe enough? | How can Ruby avoid going up and down the stairs forever? | Why does Ruby even want to go outside to play at all? | Who is going to carry Ruby up the stairs every single night?
test: What would happen if Mum tasted her tea before saying "I'm the adult, so I'd know"? | She'd find the salt and see that Leo was right | She'd prove the sugar bowl was right | She'd prove that adults are never wrong | She'd find the tea tasted much sweeter than it usually does
test: Sienna: "Dad, it's hair dye. It washes out in six weeks. I'm not joining the circus, I can't even juggle." What is true? | Each step needs its own reason; blue hair usually just means blue hair | Blue hair always leads to the circus | Dad is right that dye lasts forever | Sienna should join the circus to prove him wrong
test: Zoe: "It's a loop, Mum. You've made a loop." Is she being rude to her mum? | No, she is naming the reasoning, not insulting Mum | Yes, she is calling Mum stupid | Yes, loops are a rude word | No, she is just agreeing with everything that Mum has said
test: Jess: "Both ways work, Dad. You're not the Dishwasher King." Mum: "He is, actually, in his head." Which is true? | Some ways can be better, but the test is clean dishes | Plates must always go at the back | Mum's way must be wrong, because Dad said so and he knows dishwashers | Dishwashers only work one way
test: Big brother, rough: "Shut up asking why, it's bedtime cos it is." What kind of answer is that? | A dead end that repeats the rule instead of a reason | A clear scientific reason | A fair deal with a promise | An appeal to the weather forecast
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of the response "Because I said so" when offered in reply to the question "Why?". | It identifies the person deciding but supplies no reason | It is excessively detailed for a child | It contradicts the established bedtime | It relies too heavily upon scientific evidence that a child cannot follow
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sleep requirement of children aged nine to ten years. | Approximately nine to eleven hours nightly | Approximately five to six hours nightly | Approximately fourteen to sixteen hours nightly | No particular quantity is required
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises an argument whereby a conclusion is offered as its own justification? | Circular reasoning | An appeal to tradition | A fair agreement | A controlled experiment
exam: Determine the term properly applied to the imposition of an additional condition after the original condition of an agreement has been satisfied. | Moving the goalposts | Appeal to authority | The slippery slope | Confirmation bias
